    {
        "content": "<p>the nicer way is probably via using lemmas which state intermediate facts. e.g. reword the proof as \"if r is left total, any (nonempty) chain is a prefix of a longer chain\",  \"if r is a strict order, any chain cannot have duplicates\" and conclude that \"Therefore if r is left total and a strict order, and a nonempty chain exists, the ambient set is infinite\"</p>",
        "id": 606046590,
        "sender_full_name": "Edward van de Meent",
        "timestamp": 1782231856
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That makes sense. Do you have a suggestion for a spelling of \"chain\" that works well here?</p>",
        "id": 606059616,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1782235673
    },
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Relator</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">RightTotal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">obtain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ha</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Finite</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">wellFounded_of_trans_of_irrefl</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">has_min</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">univ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">obtain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hb</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">grind</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>you could do the right version like this, using that finite orders are well-founded. And then dualise to get the other one</p>",
        "id": 606062564,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1782236265
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is much closer to what I was imagining informally, thanks Bhavik!</p>",
        "id": 606063913,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1782236587
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think it could make sense to add that any well founded relation on a nonempty type is not right total - this is essentially a wrapped up version of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/WellFounded.html#WellFounded.not_lt_min\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/WellFounded.html#WellFounded.not_lt_min</a></p>",
